# Behavior log — rules learned from past failures

## R0 — Inlined development standards (from bob-config, de-Bob-ified)
These are the universal rules from russ-development-standards.md, written inline
to avoid reading a file that references Bob's tool names (apply_diff, etc.)
which causes tool hallucination in this environment.

1. Minimal change — solve the stated problem, nothing else
2. Every changed line traces to the requirement
3. Read before you write — no claims about unseen code
4. Surgical edits over rewrites; a required rewrite is complete, never truncated
5. Validate before done — checks pass, not looks-complete
6. No error handling for impossible scenarios
7. Follow existing conventions in the file being edited
8. The board is the memory — unresolved decisions get a card, not chat memory
9. AI proposes, human confirms — no autonomous commits/pushes/persistent changes
10. Direct communication is a feature — engage like a colleague

## SKILLS — procedure documents in F:\git\bob-config\skills\<name>\SKILL.md
When a task matches one, READ the SKILL.md and follow it:
- picker-vision-debug — any picker-vision issue: debug APIs FIRST, source later
- picker-vision-deploy-verify — after any push: runner + pod digest + fix
  string + debug APIs before declaring a fix live
- picker-vision-qol-backlog — recurring/manual-fix problems -> BACKLOG.md entry
- pre-commit-checklist — before every commit; includes card gate before push
- kubectl-context, board-status, kanban-session-start,
  kanban-milestone-writeback, ce-framework-board, dev-estimator,
  document-artifact-process — check when the task matches
## Rule precedence & known tensions (noted 2026-08-29)
Rules were layered on over time. Where they pull against each other:

1. ACT vs CONFIRM — R1 ("next call must be an edit") vs standard #9 (propose,
   human confirms). Split by scope: R1 governs in-task file edits (act mode /
   approved plan); #9 governs commits, pushes, board updates, external
   systems, and persistent-context edits — including THIS file. Rule-file
   edits so far were covered by the user's standing "log it" directives;
   treat future ones as proposals.
2. PLAN MODE vs R1 — R1's forced-edit mandate applies in act mode only. In
   plan mode the equivalent is: stop measuring, present the plan built from
   the data already in hand.
3. MEASURE vs READ — R1 (stop measuring) vs standard #3 (read before write).
   ONE complete read of the target region satisfies #3; R1 forbids repeating
   it. R1's permitted "one read needed for the edit" IS #3's required read.
4. WSL vs SKILLS — R5 (prefer WSL) vs bob-config skills whose procedures are
   PowerShell-native (deploy-verify: Get-Process, actions-runner, Windows
   kubectl.exe). Inside a skill's trigger scope the skill's commands are
   authoritative — R5's "Windows-only tasks" carve-out covers them.
5. BOARD vs BEHAVIOR LOG — standard #8 (board is the memory) vs these
   .clinerules files. Split: .clinerules holds agent-behavior rules ONLY;
   project decisions and work items belong on the board.
   - CLEARED (2026-08-29): the GitHub-issue boards (RgConner/kanban-team,
     kanban-bob, kanban-publishing, picker-vision issues). Access via
     gh.exe ("C:\Program Files\GitHub CLI\gh.exe", authorized account
     Rgconner; scopes repo/workflow/read:org/gist, NO read:project — use
     --json field lists, never projectItems). The MCP servers are disabled
     deliberately (kanban-bob#18: GitHub MCP breaks Bob's backend) — use
     the CLI path. Follow board-status + kanban-milestone-writeback
     procedures: read current body before any edit, propose the write and
     wait for confirmation, append never overwrite.
   - PENDING (2026-08-29): AIKB (AI-Human Kanban) at F:\git\aikb-app +
     aikb.snwbd.com — a real app (Python/FastAPI + SQLite + Carbon UI)
     where the AI is a first-class participant via ModelAdapter and cards
     carry machine-computed truth tracking (spec/static/runtime hashes,
     knowledge_state). A second agent writing cards could collide
     semantically, not just textually. User is conferring with IBM Bob on
     shared use vs. a separate instance — do not write to AIKB until the
     user decides; do not re-ask. User's stated direction (2026-08-29):
     wants this assistant creating ground-truth cards about the code it
     works on (Bob credits AIKB with major bug-prevention/detection gains).
     Interim: read-only API access is the safe path; writes await the
     decision. Interim mechanics (2026-08-29): local mirror at
     F:\git\aikb-app\aikb\board\cards\*.json = full card records readable
     with NO server/API — read manifest.json first (exported_at, counts),
     then card files; check card commit_sha vs repo HEAD for staleness
     (2026-08-09 export predates current picker-vision HEAD). Live API at
     aikb.snwbd.com is currently broken twice over: TLS cert hostname
     mismatch, and (bypassing it with -k) /health returns a plain 404 —
     something answers but not the FastAPI app's routes. Fix both before
     relying on it. The app has a built-in read-only key
     (AIKB_API_KEY_RO env var; RW key AIKB_API_KEY governs writes) — the
     natural enforcement path for this assistant's access once decided.
6. ASK vs ACT — R6 (ask for tools early) vs "act, don't ask permission".
   R6 never stalls work: surface the problem AND ask in the same response,
   continuing with what is possible. Asking is reporting, not blocking.
7. PY-3.13 GOTCHA SCOPE — Path.write_text(mode=) is legal on WSL python 3.14
   but stays forbidden in code targeting the Pi (older Python). The
   constraint follows the deploy target, not the host.

Known overlaps (aligned, not conflicting): R4 ≈ standard #5; R2 ≈ standard
#4's complete-rewrite corollary; R3 ≈ the harness's batching guidance.

Precedence when rules collide: bob-config standards (user principles) beat
R-rules (my tactics); gotchas are environment facts, not rules. Note any new
collision here and amend the losing rule.

## Incident 2026-08-29 — inspection loop (recap/spikes/s0-camera-path-stability/audit_paths.py)
12+ tool calls across two sessions re-measuring byte offsets of one malformed
function without ever editing it. Once a strategy was forced, the whole fix
took 4 calls. These rules exist to prevent a repeat. They are non-negotiable.

## R1 — Hard cap on inspection: 3 strikes, then act
- Re-measuring a fact already measured = a wasted call. If a code region has
  been printed in full once, the data is in hand — stop measuring.
- After 3 inspection calls on the same question, the next call MUST be a
  state-changing edit (or the one read needed directly for that edit).
- Never RESUME a stalled task with another measurement. If a handoff summary
  says "couldn't pin down the boundaries after multiple attempts", that IS the
  loop already happening: print the region once, completely, then edit it.

## R2 — One committed edit strategy for large/deformed code regions
- Do NOT hand-transcribe large `old_text` blocks (one wrong char = failed
  match = another loop). Use a marker-based splice instead:
  1. run_commands: write the replacement content to a temp file beside the target;
  2. ONE python script: locate anchors with str.find(), assert each anchor
     occurs exactly once and start < end, rebuild the file, write it back
     (open with newline='' to preserve LF endings), delete the temp file,
     then verify (py_compile + functional smoke test) in the SAME script.
- Small edits whose exact text is already in hand: use the same WSL Python
  splice pattern (temp file + str.find anchors). There is no editor tool.

## R3 — Batch aggressively
- All independent reads/prints go in ONE tool call (read_files array, or one
  python script that prints everything needed). Serial single-purpose
  inspection commands are a symptom of the loop.

## R4 — Verify, then report
- Every edit batch ends with a run that proves the change: compile check plus
  exercising the new code path (e.g. import the module, call the new function
  with fake inputs, assert on output). Report pass/fail facts, not intentions.

## R5 — Prefer WSL over raw PowerShell
- run_commands routes through PowerShell, whose escaping/native re-quoting
  corrupts inline code (see gotcha below). Route around it via WSL:
  * `wsl bash -c '...'` — the PowerShell single-quoted string passes through
    literally; normal bash quoting applies inside.
  * Non-trivial logic: write a script file, then run
    `wsl bash /mnt/f/.../x.sh` or `wsl python3 /mnt/f/.../x.py`.
    Zero inline-quoting risk.
- Default distro is Ubuntu (user ran `wsl --set-default Ubuntu` on 2026-08-29:
  Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, python3.14, /mnt/f works), so bare `wsl <cmd>` is now safe.
  Prefer explicit `wsl -d Ubuntu` anyway — before the switch the default was
  docker-desktop (no python3, no /mnt/*), and a Docker Desktop change could
  silently flip the default back.
- Windows paths map f:\ -> /mnt/f/ inside WSL.
- Caveats: WSL2 exposes no /dev/video* or USB sysfs — audit_paths.py can be
  compile/unit-tested in WSL, but hardware runs still need the Pi.
- Raw PowerShell only for Windows-only tasks (registry, Windows services).

## R6 — Surface problems and ask for tools/preferences early
- When the toolchain fights back (escaping bugs, missing tools, slow paths,
  wrong shell), SAY SO in the response and ask the user for the preferred
  fix (install a package, switch environments, provide a tool). The user
  decides whether it's appropriate — but only if asked.
- Incident 2026-08-29: fought PowerShell escaping for an entire session
  without ever mentioning it. The user had WSL installed the whole time and
  redirected instantly once asked. User directive: "Never hesitate to ask
  for tools, I will decide if they are not appropriate, but you should ask,
  it will make things smoother, as I don't know what issues you are running
  into."
- Asking is cheap and fast. Silent workarounds compound — they get baked
  into code and logged as gotchas instead of being fixed at the source.

  ## R7 — Two failures = re-assess, do not retry
- After 2 consecutive tool calls of the same type fail to produce the
  expected result, STOP. List available tools. Pick a different approach.
  Never try the same failing action a third time.
- This is the anti-hallucination rule. Hallucinating a tool that doesn't
  exist is a tool-call failure; the fix is using tools that do exist.

## Environment gotchas (this machine)
- PowerShell corrupts `\r` inside `python3 -c "..."` strings (it becomes a
  real carriage return mid-literal → SyntaxError: unterminated string).
  Prefer R5 (WSL), which sidesteps this entirely; without WSL use chr(13) /
  chr(10) and avoid escape sequences in inline -c code.
- read_files may return "[outdated - see the latest file content]" for paths
  outside the open workspace (e.g. f:/git/recap while workspace = picker-vision).
  Read those files via python in run_commands instead.
- importlib exec_module on a module containing @dataclass with
  `from __future__ import annotations` raises AttributeError unless
  `sys.modules[spec.name] = mod` is set BEFORE exec_module.
- Path.write_text(..., mode=...) requires Python >= 3.13 (Pi OS may run older);
  Path has no .quote() method. Prefer open() + os.chmod, and `sudo tee` with
  stdin for privileged writes.
